+Finally, if you have set the conditional values, you can evaluate the
+request's preconditions against the response.  Set at least an ETag or
+``last_modified``, then call :meth:`~Response.apply_conditions` with the
+request object.  If the request shows that the client already has the current
+data, the status is changed to 304 and no body is sent, which saves traffic.
+If a precondition such as ``If-Match`` fails, a 412
+:exc:`~werkzeug.exceptions.PreconditionFailed` error is raised.
+
+After that, call :meth:`~Response.apply_range` to handle a ``Range`` header.
+If the range can be satisfied, the response becomes a 206 partial response
+with only the selected bytes.
+
+>>> response.apply_conditions(request)
+>>> response.apply_range(request)
+
+``Response.make_conditional`` did both in one step, but is deprecated.
 
 
